.TH FETCH_LLM 1 "2026-08-10" "ragfarm" "ragfarm LLM life-cycle"
.SH NAME
fetch_llm.py \- download, verify and register LLM checkpoints for ragfarm
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scripts/fetch_llm.py
.B -m
.I HF_REPO
.RB [ --alias
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ --preset
.IR ID ]
.RB [ --display
.IR LABEL ]
.RB [ --profile
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ --comment
.IR TEXT ]
.PP
.B scripts/fetch_llm.py --sync
.RB [ --yes ]
.br
.B scripts/fetch_llm.py --verify
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fetch_llm.py
is the first of the three LLM life-cycle tools. It puts a checkpoint on disk,
proves the copy is complete, and records it in the registry so that
.BR activate_llm (1)
can bind it to a slot. It is the
.B only
supported way to add a model: a directory dropped into
.I models/llm/
by hand has no registry entry, no verified size, and therefore no way to size
the GPU budget.
.PP
A model becomes usable in three stages, and this tool owns the first two:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fetch_llm.py   ->  on disk + in downloaded[]   ("we have it")
activate_llm.py ->  bound to a slot, unit running ("it is resident")
Open WebUI     ->  preset bound to its alias    ("a human can pick it")
.fi
.RE
.SS Downloads are verified, then registered
The registry entry is appended
.B after
the transfer succeeds and every file matches the Hub's size metadata. A failed
or interrupted download leaves nothing in
.IR active.json ,
so a half-model can never be activated by accident.
.PP
On success the verified total is stored as
.BR size_gib .
That number, not
.BR du ,
is what
.BR activate_llm (1)
uses to compute the slot's memory share \- a partially downloaded checkpoint
measures small and would silently under-allocate.
.SS Why curl and not huggingface_hub
On this network
.B huggingface_hub
hangs indefinitely on sockets that die mid-transfer.
.B HF_HUB_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT
does not fire, the process stays alive at 0 B/s, and control never returns to a
retry loop. The cause is provider-side rate limiting rather than a dropped link:
the connection stays open and simply stops delivering, so reconnecting on error
never triggers because there is no error.
.PP
.B curl
with
.BR --speed-limit / --speed-time
detects exactly that condition \- socket open, delivering nothing \- aborts, and
resumes with
.B -C -
without losing bytes. Measured 2026-08-04:
.B huggingface_hub
stalled twice and lost progress;
.B curl
completed 67 GB across several dropouts. The Hub API is still used, but only for
metadata: the file list and per-file sizes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -m ", " --model " " \fIHF_REPO\fR
Hugging Face repo id, e.g.
.IR RedHatAI/Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct-FP8-dynamic .
Registers and downloads it. Re-running with a repo already in the registry is a
no-op, so the command is safe to repeat.
.TP
.B --alias " " \fINAME\fR
The served name. vLLM advertises it on
.IR /v1/models ,
and Open WebUI binds its preset to it. Defaults to the lowercased directory
name. Must be unique.
.TP
.B --preset " " \fIID\fR
The Open WebUI preset id this model gets. Defaults to
.IR ragfarm-<dirname> .
Must be unique.
.TP
.B --display " " \fILABEL\fR
Human-readable label in the model picker. Free text; put the useful facts in it
(size, quantisation, dense or MoE), because this is what someone reads at
demo time.
.TP
.B --profile " " \fINAME\fR
Which
.B MODEL_TUNING
profile in
.I infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
supplies the system prompt, sampler and capabilities. Currently
.B vision-thinking
or
.BR vision-instruct .
Default:
.BR vision-thinking .
.IP
.B Get this right.
It is not cosmetic. It selects the sampling values, and
.BR activate_llm (1)
also reads it to decide whether the slot gets
.BR --reasoning-parser .
Give a non-thinking checkpoint the thinking profile and the parser files the
model's entire answer as "reasoning": the chat message comes back
.B empty
with the whole reply hidden in the collapsed thinking trace. That is
convincingly mistakable for a broken model or a bad quantisation. Observed
2026-08-09.
.TP
.B --comment " " \fITEXT\fR
Free-text note stored in the registry. Use it for the thing the next person will
wish they knew: "dense, fits alone only", "community quant, unverified", "needs
KV 16".
.TP
.B --sync
Reconcile disk with the registry. Downloads anything in
.B downloaded[]
that is missing or fails verification, backfills
.B size_gib
for entries that predate that field, and
.B reports
directories under
.I models/llm/
that no registry entry claims.
.TP
.B --yes
Only meaningful with
.BR --sync :
actually delete the unclaimed directories. Without it, deletions are a dry run
and merely printed. A directory referenced by
.B active[]
is refused even with
.BR --yes .
.TP
.B --verify
Re-check every registered model on disk against the Hub. Changes nothing. This
is the honest answer to "did that download actually finish?", and it is worth
running before you plan an activation around a model you fetched overnight.
.SH UNIQUENESS IS CHECKED BEFORE THE DOWNLOAD
.BR activate_llm (1)
refuses to operate at all on a registry containing a duplicate
.BR model ,
.B alias
or
.B preset
\- deliberately, because choosing which name wins is a decision only a human
should make, and silently disambiguating would produce a picker whose entries do
not mean what their names say.
.PP
So this tool checks uniqueness
.B first,
before a byte is transferred. Discovering the clash after a 70 GB, multi-hour
download would be bad enough; worse, the duplicate would then be
.I in
the registry, blocking every other slot operation until fixed by hand.
.PP
When it refuses, pass explicit names rather than editing
.I active.json
afterwards.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Add a model with everything named explicitly
.RS
.nf
scripts/fetch_llm.py -m RedHatAI/Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct-FP8-dynamic \\
    --alias qwen25-vl-72b-instruct-fp8 \\
    --preset ragfarm-vision-72b \\
    --display 'Qwen2.5-VL 72B Instruct FP8 (dense)' \\
    --profile vision-instruct \\
    --comment 'dense 72B; fits alone only -- clear the other slot first'
.fi
.RE
.SS Rebuild a machine from the registry
On a fresh box with an empty
.IR models/llm/ ,
this is the whole restore:
.RS
.nf
scripts/fetch_llm.py --sync
.fi
.RE
.SS Check what is actually complete
.RS
.nf
scripts/fetch_llm.py --verify
.fi
.RE
.SS Reclaim disk from models you have retired
.RS
.nf
scripts/fetch_llm.py --sync            # dry run: prints what it WOULD delete
scripts/fetch_llm.py --sync --yes      # do it
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remove the entry from
.B downloaded[]
first \- that is what makes the directory unclaimed. Anything still referenced
by
.B active[]
is refused regardless.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I models/llm/active.json
The registry this tool writes. See
.BR active.json (1).
.TP
.I models/llm/<model>/
Checkpoint directories, named after the repo's last path segment.
.TP
.I models/llm/MODEL.md
The human record: what each model is for and what it measured. Not machine-read.
Update it when you add a model \- the registry holds names and sizes, not
judgement.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything requested is on disk and verified.
.TP
.B 1
A download failed, a name clashed, or
.B --verify
found something absent or incomplete. On a name clash nothing was downloaded and
nothing was written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR activate_llm (1),
.BR active.json (1),
.BR stack (1),
.BR setup_openwebui (1),
.BR env (1)
.PP
.I models/llm/MODEL.md
for the per-model record;
.I docs/decisions/ADR-0013
for why the engine split cares which quantisation you fetch.
.SH NOTES
Downloads are large and slow. Run them in a detached session; the resume logic
means an interrupted transfer costs time, not bytes, but it cannot resume a
process you killed at the shell.
